---
epr-meta-version: 1
covers: subtree
rules:
  - id: durable-primitives-are-native-py
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.py"
      contains-any: [".jsonl", "datetime.now(", "time.time(", ">= hard", "> hard", ">= soft",
                     "\"hard\"", "'hard'", "ceiling"]
    dedupe-of: "elohim/eprfs/epr-cli/src/flow/{sense,report,concern}.rs + elohim/epr-rea/src/model.rs (the native fold writer, judge, concern resolver and limit model)"
    why: >
      Python in this tree is a disposable local artifact of the design (operator ruling
      2026-10-22: the lowest-level primitives have first-class Rust support for performance and
      durability). A script that keeps a ledger (`.jsonl` with its own cursor and closure rule),
      judges a level against a watermark, or dates a measurement by the wall clock is growing a
      primitive in the disposable layer. The durable homes: a finding is a fold written by
      `epr flow sense` (a `probe:` on the measure row — `native:` for a primitive, `run:` only
      for a detector still being learned); a level is judged by `elohim_epr_rea::Bound` or the
      native report (`epr flow report --bound`, `--breaches`); a concern reference resolves through
      `flow::concern`; notes are dated by the git HEAD, not `now()`. Census: the 2026-10-22
      substrate-bypass survey (16 private ledgers, a habit-side regex copy of the concern tag,
      seven wall-clock reads) — spec genesis/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-22-sense-organ-design.md.
      If this script is a deliberate sketch, leave it so and say what retires it. (Advisory
      `inject` nudge — never blocks, names no validator.)
    retire-when: "when no .claude/data/*.jsonl appender remains and limit-models-open@1 reads 0 — the dev system's primitives are then native by construction"
---
`.claude/scripts/` — the agentic memory + governance toolkit (the `_lib` cascade, the memory-kit
audit/stasis tools, the lint harness). This manifest CLAIMS full responsibility for the subtree
(`covers: subtree`): the coverage walk terminates here, integrity by construction — the way the core
never re-audits an app-manifest's vocabulary (seam-map §3.7).

**Considered, no enforcing rule yet (the deliberate opt-in, not a nag).** Edit-time governance of this
tree is already carried by the managed-surface PreToolUse hook (`managed-surface-context.py`) and the
`_lib/__tests__/*_test.py` convention (a new `_lib` module ships its runnable test). Those are the real,
already-mechanized gates — adding a `.epr-meta` rule that fired on the same writes would be the
"fires-on-nothing" footgun this very toolkit's `validate_meta` guards against. So this is the
considered-coverage outcome: responsibility owned, no redundant rule. Add a rule here only if a NEW
recurring, mechanizable drift appears in this tree.

This is the first worked example of the epr-meta coverage campaign (`placement-audit.py --epr-meta`):
the tree that built the coverage signal owns itself first.
